The longer I work in delivery, the less I care about the things I used to think were really important!

Experience has a habit of showing you what matters and here are a few I’ve gradually let go of:

Following a framework properly

I care much more about whether the team can deliver something valuable than whether we can point to the bit of the guide or manual that says we’re doing it right.

Having a perfect plan

Plans are useful. Pretending reality will conform to them usually isn’t.

Velocity

I’d much rather understand whether work is flowing, whether we’re learning and whether the thing we’re building is useful than how many points we think it's worth.

Looking busy

You can fill a calendar, produce reports, update tickets and still move absolutely nothing forward.

Always having the answer

A big part of delivery is knowing when you don’t have it, asking the right question and getting the right people in the room to crack the really tough questions.

Staying in your lane

Delivery problems rarely fit neatly inside a job description. If something is getting in the way, I’m going to get involved.

Being right first time

I’ve become much more comfortable changing my mind when new information shows up (And understanding I know much less than what I don't know!).

I don’t think experience naturally makes delivery simpler but it makes you realise how much of it comes down to judgement, context and trade-offs and saying or thinking it depends more on than anyone likes.

